§ `VerifyWrittenGuardsFixture`

-- Negative controls: conditions the drafter DID write that exclude nothing
-- (smucclaw/l4-ide#1093). Each is reported once, as a `vacuous-guard`, at the
-- place it sits in the rule as written.
--
-- verify-vacuous-guard.l4 has the plainest case, `x AND (x OR y)`, whose
-- normal form is the rule itself. These are the cases where the normal form
-- and the rule as written come apart.

-- (1) The second condition is redundant, and its normal form is two clauses,
--     `x OR q` and `x OR r`. One finding, at the condition, not one per clause.
GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN, q IS A BOOLEAN, r IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in two parts` x q r IF
    x AND (x OR (q AND r))

-- (2) The redundant condition is inside one limb of a disjunction. The rule
--     as written has no conjunction at the top; its normal form does.
GIVEN p IS A BOOLEAN, q IS A BOOLEAN, r IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle inside a limb` p q r IF
       (p AND (p OR q))
    OR r

-- (3) The redundant condition is inside the requirement of a rule with a
--     scope, which is read in the context of that scope.
GIVEN s IS A BOOLEAN, x IS A BOOLEAN, y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in the requirement` s x y IF
    s IMPLIES (x AND (x OR y))

-- (4) The redundant condition is in what a row of a BRANCH says, which is
--     checked like any other condition the drafter wrote.
GIVEN s IS A BOOLEAN, x IS A BOOLEAN, y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in a row` s x y IF
  BRANCH IF s THEN x AND (x OR y)
         OTHERWISE FALSE

-- (5) The redundant condition is the first word of a bracket inside another
--     bracket. Its place is that word, not the bracket before it, which opens
--     the group around it.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in two brackets` a b c IF
    a AND ((a AND b) OR c)

-- (6) The redundant condition is in a local definition with a parameter, and
--     its first word is the parameter. Read through, that word is the argument
--     at the call; the condition is placed in the definition, where it is
--     written, once for each use.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, d IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in a definition` a b d IF
    `qualifies` a AND `qualifies` b
  WHERE
    GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN
    GIVETH A BOOLEAN
    `qualifies` x MEANS x AND (x OR d)

-- (7) The redundant condition is in what the ELSE of an IF says, which is
--     checked like what a THEN says.
GIVEN g IS A BOOLEAN, x IS A BOOLEAN, y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in an ELSE` g x y IF
    IF g THEN FALSE ELSE x AND (x OR y)

-- (8) The redundant condition is in what the OTHERWISE of a BRANCH says.
GIVEN g IS A BOOLEAN, x IS A BOOLEAN, y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in an OTHERWISE` g x y IF
  BRANCH IF g THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE x AND (x OR y)

-- (9) The redundant condition is a call, which reads through to a condition
--     the rule already asks for: `either a b` means `a OR b`. It is placed
--     at the call.
GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN, y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `either` x y IF x OR y

GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle through a call` a b IF
    a AND `either` a b

-- Not reported.

-- (10) The redundant condition is in the test of a row. Every row after the
--      first carries the tests before it, negated, which nobody wrote, so the
--      tests are not checked; this is what that costs.
GIVEN g IS A BOOLEAN, x IS A BOOLEAN, y IS A BOOLEAN, z IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in a test` g x y z IF
  BRANCH IF x AND (x OR y) THEN g
         OTHERWISE z

-- Reported once, as a dead branch, not also as a redundant condition.

-- (11) Under a NOT, a condition the context entails is, read the other way
--      round, an alternative that cannot hold: `a AND NOT (a AND b)` is
--      `a AND ((NOT a) OR (NOT b))`, whose `NOT a` is dead.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle under a NOT` a b IF
    a AND NOT (a AND b)

-- (12) The same, deeper: inside an alternative under the NOT.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a hurdle in an alternative under a NOT` a b c IF
    a AND NOT (b OR (a AND c))
